<Base title="Extra Pass: Modeling Missed Opportunities | Bence Supola"><div class="container"><div class="case-hero"><div class="eyebrow">Case study 03 · New KPI / Counterfactual simulation</div><h1>Can We Measure a Missed Extra Pass?</h1><p class="lead">Operationalizing a subjective basketball skill: identifying when passing up an immediate shot might have created a higher-value shot for a teammate.</p><div class="skills"><span class="skill">New KPI design</span><span class="skill">Counterfactual simulation</span><span class="skill">Quantile regression</span><span class="skill">Expected-points modeling</span></div></div><div class="stats"><div class="stat"><strong>15,479</strong><span>Eligible assisted half-court shots</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>0.15 / 0.50</strong><span>Pass-duration quantiles compared</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>19.98–34.06%</strong><span>Model-estimated extra-pass opportunities in the selected actions</span></div></div><div class="prose"><h2>The main problem: a valued skill with no reliable measure</h2><p>Coaches often praise the “extra pass”: declining a decent, immediate shot to give a teammate an even better one. But the term had no sufficiently precise definition for automated assessment, and conventional statistics only describe passes that were actually made. The research therefore set out to <strong>define the action and build a reproducible way to evaluate missed opportunities</strong>.</p><p>We restricted the question deliberately: the alternative teammate had to be a plausible <em>immediate</em> recipient at the moment the shooter received the ball. This is not a system for predicting creative future plays, player intentions, or every possible option that could develop several seconds later.</p></div><div class="prose"><h2>A defensible counterfactual starts with feasible alternatives</h2><p>Starting from the earlier event-extraction work, we selected <strong>15,479</strong> assisted half-court shooting actions in which the shot began within 0.5 seconds of receiving the ball. For each situation, we asked whether a different teammate could plausibly receive an immediate extra pass and obtain a better shot.</p><p>First, a <strong>pass-congestion metric</strong> represented how defenders obstructed the lane between passer and receiver. The method weighted defenders according to their position along the lane and used observed successful passes and turnovers to set a conservative feasibility threshold. The threshold deliberately avoided recommending marginal passes when the player already had a reasonable shot.</p></div><div class="prose"><h2>The timing problem: defenders move while the ball travels</h2><p>An open receiver at the start of a pass may no longer be open when the ball arrives. To estimate the likely contest, the simulation needed both <strong>pass duration</strong> and <strong>defender closeout movement</strong>. Pass duration was modeled from pass distance; closeout speed was estimated separately from the defender-to-shooter distance. Together these estimates informed the nearest-defender distance at the hypothetical shot.</p><p>The observed pass durations were highly variable at the same distance. A single mean prediction could overstate the time available to close out, so the paper used <strong>second-order quantile regression</strong>. The 0.15 quantile represented a faster-pass scenario and the median (0.50) a slower boundary. The faster setting was tuned against expected-versus-observed points in a separate subset of assisted shots.</p><p>This component estimates a physical timing input for the simulation. <strong>It does not predict the player’s decision, the next play or whether the hypothetical pass would actually succeed.</strong></p></div><figure class="figure figure-wide"><a href="/images/extra-pass-duration.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><img src="/images/extra-pass-duration.png" alt="Two scatterplots of observed air and dribble pass durations against distance with orange quadratic trend curves." loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><strong>Pass duration versus distance.</strong> Unpublished exploratory plots for air and dribble passes. The orange curves show quadratic trend fits; they should not be identified as the published quantile-regression curves without checking their fitting procedure. The broad scatter explains why distance alone is an imperfect timing predictor.</figcaption></figure><div class="prose"><h2>Turning a possible pass into a comparable scoring value</h2><p>For each candidate destination, the model estimated shot quality using the shooting area, distance to the nearest defender at the simulated release and the relevant shooter’s historical performance. This produced <strong>expected points</strong> for the hypothetical shot, which could be compared with the expected points of the shot actually taken.</p><p>To avoid treating small estimated differences as decisive, the analysis examined systematic expected-points errors across court areas and applied a correction when judging whether an alternative was reliably better. This step matters: a counterfactual recommendation is only as credible as its feasibility assumptions and shot-value calibration.</p></div><div class="prose"><h2>A new evaluation metric — with explicit limits</h2><p>The model could flag situations in which an extra pass appeared preferable under its assumptions and aggregate them by player and court area. In the selected assisted half-court actions, its estimated extra-pass opportunities ranged from <strong>19.98% to 34.06%</strong> across the modeled pass-duration scenarios. This is a model-dependent range, <strong>not</strong> an observed rate of wrong player decisions.</p><p>The contribution is a new operational framework for examining a largely unmeasured aspect of ball movement. It combines event extraction, geometry, statistical timing models, defensive-response assumptions, shot-value estimation and conservative comparisons. It can guide film review and generate hypotheses; it cannot establish what would certainly have happened after a pass that was never played.</p></div><div class="prose"><h2>My role and scope</h2><p>I implemented the programming and analytical workflow underpinning this collaborative research. The study design, scientific interpretation and publication were developed with my coauthors.</p><p><a href="https://doi.org/10.2478/IJCSS-2023-0002">Read the peer-reviewed paper ↗</a> · <a href="/#work">Back to selected work</a></p></div></div></Base>
